Subtask 3.2: Create and Execute the Test Playbook
Create the testing and validation playbook:
cat > playbooks/test-apache.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Test Apache HTTP Server Installation and Configuration
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars_files:
    - ../vars/vhosts.yml
  vars:
    apache_service_name: httpd

  tasks:
    - name: Check Apache service status
      systemd:
        name: "{{ apache_service_name }}"
      register: apache_status
      tags: service_check

    - name: Display Apache service status
      debug:
        msg: "Active: {{ apache_status.status.ActiveState }}, Running: {{ apache_status.status.SubState }}"
      tags: service_check

    - name: Check if Apache is listening on port 80
      wait_for:
        port: 80
        host: "{{ ansible_default_ipv4.address }}"
        timeout: 10
      tags: port_check

    - name: Test HTTP connectivity to default site
      uri:
        url: "http://{{ ansible_default_ipv4.address }}"
        method: GET
        status_code: 200
      register: http_test
      tags: http_test

    - name: Test virtual hosts HTTP connectivity
      uri:
        url: "http://{{ ansible_default_ipv4.address }}"
        method: GET
        headers:
          Host: "{{ item.server_name }}"
        status_code: 200
      loop: "{{ virtual_hosts }}"
      register: vhost_http_test
      tags: vhost_test

    - name: Test custom 404 error page
      uri:
        url: "http://{{ ansible_default_ipv4.address }}/nonexistent-page"
        method: GET
        status_code: 404
      register: error_page_test
      tags: error_test

    - name: Check Apache configuration syntax
      command: httpd -t
      register: config_syntax
      changed_when: false
      tags: syntax_check

    - name: Generate test report
      template:
        src: ../templates/test-report.html.j2
        dest: /var/www/html/test-report.html
        owner: apache
        group: apache
        mode: '0644'
      tags: test_report
EOF
Execute the test playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/test-apache.yml
Deliverable: The play recap shows failed=0 for both hosts. The debug task prints Active: active, Running: running for each server.

Subtask 3.3: Validate Results and Interpret the Test Report
Fetch and view the generated test report from the control node (replace with your actual target IP):
curl -s http://10.0.1.10/test-report.html | grep -A2 "Check</th>"
Confirm the custom error page is served correctly:
curl -s http://10.0.1.10/nonexistent-page | grep "404"
Interpret the results:
If http_test.status is 200, the default site and virtual hosts are reachable.
If the error_page_test task passed with status_code: 404, the custom error page configuration in httpd.conf is active and correctly routes unknown URLs.
If config_syntax.stdout or config_syntax.stderr contains Syntax OK, the Apache configuration is valid and safe to reload.
Deliverable: The curl command against /nonexistent-page returns the custom 404 page content (the text "404" appears in the output) rather than a generic Apache error page, and the test report page at /test-report.html renders a table showing active for the Apache service.

Troubleshooting
Common issues you may encounter during this lab:

Apache fails to start or httpd -t reports a syntax error

Symptom: systemctl status httpd shows failed, or the restart apache handler causes the playbook to fail.
Diagnostic hint: Run ansible webservers -i inventory/hosts.yml -b -m command -a "httpd -t" to see the exact configuration error and file/line number, then check the corresponding .conf file in /etc/httpd/conf.d/ for typos such as unmatched <Directory> blocks.
curl returns connection refused or times out on port 80

Symptom: curl -I http://<host> hangs or returns Connection refused.
Diagnostic hint: Check whether the firewall is blocking the port with ansible webservers -i inventory/hosts.yml -b -m command -a "firewall-cmd --list-services", and confirm SELinux is not blocking Apache with ansible webservers -i inventory/hosts.yml -b -m command -a "getenforce".
Virtual host returns the default site instead of expected content

Symptom: Requesting example1.local via the Host header still returns the default index.html.
Diagnostic hint: Verify the virtual host file was created in /etc/httpd/conf.d/ and that Apache was actually restarted after the change (check the handler ran by looking for a changed status on the restart apache handler in the playbook output).
ansible -m ping fails with UNREACHABLE

Symptom: Ansible cannot connect to web1 or web2.
Diagnostic hint: Confirm the IP addresses in inventory/hosts.yml match the actual assigned addresses for your lab environment, and test raw SSH connectivity manually with ssh centos@<ip>.
Lab Cleanup
If you need to remove the lab resources and reset the target servers, run the following from the control node:

ansible -i inventory/hosts.yml webservers -b -m yum -a "name=httpd,mod_ssl,httpd-tools state=absent"
ansible -i inventory/hosts.yml webservers -b -m file -a "path=/var/www/example1 state=absent"
ansible -i inventory/hosts.yml webservers -b -m file -a "path=/var/www/example2 state=absent"
ansible -i inventory/hosts.yml webservers -b -m file -a "path=/var/www/error-pages state=absent"
This removes Apache and the virtual host content created during the lab. The project files on the control node (~/apache-automation) can be removed with rm -rf ~/apache-automation if no longer needed.

